Hashing in DSA - Full Beginner Guide
(English + Hinglish Explanation)

What is Hashing?
Hashing is a technique used to map data to a fixed-size value using a hash function.
(Hashing ek technique hai jo kisi bhi data ko ek fixed-size value me convert karti hai hash function ka use karke.)

It is widely used in:
✅ Data structures like HashMap, HashSet
✅ Database indexing
✅ Cryptography (password hashing)
✅ Caching and searching algorithms

Why Use Hashing?
Fast Searching – Hashing allows O(1) average time complexity for searching.
(Searching bahut fast hoti hai, average case me O(1) time lagta hai.)
Efficient Storage – Instead of storing data in lists, hashing stores data in a structured way.
(Hashing data ko efficiently store karta hai, list ki tarah linear search nahi karni padti.)
Avoids Duplicate Data – HashSet uses hashing to store only unique values.
(HashSet duplicate values store nahi karta, kyunki hashing ka use karta hai.)
Fast Data Access – Hashing helps in quickly retrieving data from large datasets.
(Bade datasets me data ko jaldi retrieve karne ke liye hashing use hota hai.)
How Hashing Works?
Hash Function: Converts an input (key) into a fixed-size value (hash code/index).
(Hash function input ko ek fixed-size index me convert karta hai.)
Hash Table: An array where the values are stored at the index calculated by the hash function.
(Hash Table ek array hota hai, jisme values hash function ke index par store hoti hain.)
Collision Handling: If two keys produce the same hash index, a technique is used to handle it.
(Agar do keys ka same index aata hai, to collision handling ka use hota hai.)
Example of Hashing
Suppose we have names:
["John", "David", "Mark", "Lisa"]

A hash function can assign them indices like:

